          "text": "{\"translation_en\":\"NORA. Oh, it hurts me so much, Torvald, because you have always been so kind to me. But I cannot help it. **I do not love you anymore.**\",\"larger_context_summary\":\"Near the end of the play, Nora speaks directly to her husband, Torvald, during their climactic reckoning after his response to Krogstad’s letter has exposed to her the limitations of their marriage. Although Torvald now tries to preserve their relationship, Nora has concluded that they have lived as strangers within a household structured around his control and her performance as his “doll.” She acknowledges his kindness and regrets hurting him, but presents her changed feeling as something she cannot alter. The disclosure forms part of her considered explanation for leaving both Torvald and their home in order to educate herself and discover an independent identity.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The crucial semantic unit is the entire sentence, not the positively worded substring caught inside it: Nora says that she no longer loves Torvald. The negation and temporal “anymore” make this a strong truth-claim about the cessation of an earlier emotional state, which explains the dominant truth-conditional force. Her sorrowful preface gives the statement emotional gravity, but intensity is not the same as reflexive or expressive force: she speaks deliberately, supplies reasons, and says she cannot change the fact rather than simply crying out. The declaration has enormous consequences for the marriage and helps articulate Nora’s decision to leave, yet those consequences do not make the sentence performative in itself. She is neither promising nor accomplishing the separation merely by uttering these words. This occurrence is especially valuable because a substring search could misleadingly extract “I love you”; the full syntax reverses that apparent meaning.\"}"
        }
